  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 333 AM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nExtensive low stratus has developed overnight across MN, western\nWI, southward into NE and MO thanks to rich moisture advection \naround 925 mb. The cloud depth is only about 1 kft, so the\nclouds should lift and scatter relatively quickly this morning.\nOnce the clouds scatter, temperatures will climb into the low to\nmid 90s this afternoon across MN and upper 80s in WI. Combined\nwith dew points in the 65-70 degree range across central MN and\nnorthwest WI to the 75-80 degree range across southern MN, heat\nindices will peak in the 95-100 degree range across central MN\nand western WI, to around 105 across south central and west\ncentral MN. Extreme Heat Warnings begin this afternoon from the\nmetro south and west, with a Heat Advisory across central MN and\nfar western WI. Rising heights and increasing 700 mb\ntemperatures should put a lid on thunderstorm development this\nafternoon in most areas, but a few storms may fire in parts of\nWI where capping is weaker. However, the lack of clear forcing\nmay too prevent much from developing. Understandably, CAMs are \nnot too optimistic today. If a storm does develop, it will have\nplenty of deep layer shear and instability to work with, so the\nsevere risk today is conditional. Additional storms may fire\nover central and western SD this afternoon where very hot \ntemperatures of 105-110 are predicted. Should they organize and\ndevelop a cold pool, they may track into southwestern MN late\nthis evening or overnight. \n\nThe impacts of any overnight convection do raise some questions\nas to how hot highs will get Sunday. Mid 90s are forecast \nacross the region which should be easily doable if the cold pool\nwashes out quickly. If not, it may be a few degrees cooler, but\ndew points may be even higher. ECMWF and Canadian models are \nadvertising low 80s across much of southern and western MN. Even\nwith highs in the lower 90s, lower 80s dew points will still \nresult in heat indices nearing 115. Confidence is high the\nwarning will verify, even if confidence in high temperatures is\nlower. Expanded the warning north and east for Sunday, but kept\nthe watch going from Douglas to Rusk counties where it remains \nmarginal. \n\nA diffuse cold front will push southeast Sunday night. Forcing\nalong the front is very weak, but a strengthening LLJ Sunday\nevening could be a compensating forcing mechanism. 700 mb temps\nremain very warm (+12 to +15C) which is usually prohibitively\nhigh to support convective development. Nonetheless, some \nmodels, including the ECMWF, insist on convection developing\nSunday evening and diving south into IA overnight. This MCS\nwould be capable of wind damage should it form. Confidence\nremains low.\n\nDrier air will work in Monday which is expected to keep heat \nindices below warning criteria. Depending how warm highs can \nget, a Heat Advisory may eventually be necessary. \n\nNorthwest flow continues all next week. Ripples embedded within\nthe flow could bring chances for thunderstorms at any point, but\nthese are not predictable at this range. Temperatures will\nremain very warm with several days in the low 90s expected.",
